{
  "dom_id": "step-18885",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
